      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "I have the full problem text. Let me work through the mathematics carefully.\n\n**Problem:** Function $x = x(y)$ satisfies $y = \\int \\frac{4y^3}{4-y^6}\\,dx$, curve $L$: $x=x(y)$ for $-2 \\le y \\le -1$, with $x(-1) = -\\frac{9}{16}$. Find (1) arc length $s$; (2) maximum value of $x$.\n\n**Setting up the ODE.** The relation $y = \\int \\frac{4y^3}{4-y^6}\\,dx$ means $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{4y^3}{4-y^6}$ (differentiating w.r.t. $x$). So $\\frac{dx}{dy} = \\frac{4-y^6}{4y^3} = \\frac{1}{y^3} - \\frac{y^3}{4}$.\n\n**Integrating:** $x(y) = \\int\\left(\\frac{1}{y^3} - \\frac{y^3}{4}\\right)dy = -\\frac{1}{2y^2} - \\frac{y^4}{16} + C$.\n\nUsing $x(-1) = -\\frac{9}{16}$: $-\\frac{1}{2(1)} - \\frac{1}{16} + C = -\\frac{9}{16} \\Rightarrow -\\frac{8}{16} - \\frac{1}{16} + C = -\\frac{9}{16} \\Rightarrow C = 0$.\n\nSo $x(y) = -\\frac{1}{2y^2} - \\frac{y^4}{16}$.\n\n**(1) Arc length.** $s = \\int_{-2}^{-1}\\sqrt{1 + (x'(y))^2}\\,dy$ with $x'(y) = \\frac{1}{y^3} - \\frac{y^3}{4}$.\n\n$1 + (x')^2 = 1 + \\frac{1}{y^6} - \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{y^6}{16} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{y^6} + \\frac{y^6}{16} = \\left(\\frac{1}{y^3} + \\frac{y^3}{4}\\right)^2$.\n\nSince $y \\in [-2,-1]$, $y^3 < 0$, so $\\frac{1}{y^3} + \\frac{y^3}{4} < 0$, hence $\\sqrt{1+(x')^2} = -\\left(\\frac{1}{y^3} + \\frac{y^3}{4}\\right)$.\n\n$s = \\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(-\\frac{1}{y^3} - \\frac{y^3}{4}\\right)dy = \\left[\\frac{1}{2y^2} - \\frac{y^4}{16}\\right]_{-2}^{-1}$.\n\nAt $y=-1$: $\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{16} = \\frac{7}{16}$. At $y=-2$: $\\frac{1}{8} - \\frac{16}{16} = \\frac{1}{8} - 1 = -\\frac{7}{8}$.\n\n$s = \\frac{7}{16} - \\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right) = \\frac{7}{16} + \\frac{14}{16} = \\frac{21}{16}$.\n\n**(2) Maximum of $x$.** $x(y) = -\\frac{1}{2y^2} - \\frac{y^4}{16}$ on $[-2,-1]$. $x'(y) = \\frac{1}{y^3} - \\frac{y^3}{4} = \\frac{4 - y^6}{4y^3}$. On $[-2,-1]$, $y^6 \\in [1,64]$, so $4 - y^6 < 0$ and $y^3 < 0$, giving $x' > 0$: $x$ is increasing on $[-2,-1]$. Maximum at $y = -1$: $x(-1) = -\\frac{9}{16}$.\n\nLet me verify the arc length numerically and submit.",
